<Article
  title="The Owner’s Position"
  heading="The owner’s position"
  kicker="Cornerstone"
  description="Why owners on large residential projects are structurally disadvantaged, where the leverage actually sits, and what independent representation changes."
>
  <p>
    Commission a significant residence and you become, briefly, the chief executive of a small and expensive
    company. It has a payroll, a supply chain, a design office and a burn rate. You did not choose the role,
    you will hold it for two or three years, and almost everything you know about how the company is doing
    will come to you from people you are paying.
  </p>

  <h2>Who works for whom</h2>
  <p>
    There are no villains in this account. The general contractor’s project manager is competent, probably
    honest, and employed by the contractor: when scope is ambiguous, their obligation runs to the company
    paying their salary. The architect’s deepest loyalty is to the design — they will defend the drawings
    before they defend your contingency. The engineers protect their stamps, as they should. The lender’s
    inspector protects the loan collateral. Your lawyer reads the contract once, before signing, and returns
    to their practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each of these people is doing their job well. The difficulty is that nobody at the table has, as their
    job, your whole position — cost, schedule, quality and risk, held together, on your side only. The
    structure works far more often than it fails. When it fails, it fails expensively and late, and the owner
    is the party holding the result.
  </p>

  <Plate
    src="/plates/plate-05-linework.svg"
    alt="Close crop of survey contour linework and dimension marks from a technical drawing"
    caption="Drawings are promises. The contract decides what they cost."
    class="my-12 aspect-[16/9]"
  />

  <h2>What the owner cannot see</h2>
  <p>
    The common advice is to hire a more honest builder. It misses the point: the things that cost owners
    money are mostly invisible from the owner’s chair regardless of anyone’s character, because the parties
    who can see them have no particular reason to show you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Buyout margins.</strong> After the contract is signed, the contractor buys out the work —
    committing subcontractors against the prices carried in the bid. Where the buyout beats the bid, the
    difference is margin; whether any of it reaches you depends entirely on contract language almost nobody
    negotiates. You will never see the buyout unless the contract makes you entitled to.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Whether the schedule is real.</strong> A schedule is only as honest as its logic — sequence,
    long-lead orders, permit assumptions. A date without logic behind it is a hope, and hopes get quietly
    re-baselined. Owners are shown dates; they are rarely shown logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Whether a change order was in scope.</strong> Some changes are genuinely new work. Others are
    items that arguably belonged in the contract price, returning with a markup. Telling one from the other
    requires reading the change against the documents — line by line, every time — which is exactly the work
    nobody at the table is paid by you to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What a payment application actually represents.</strong> It is a claim, not a fact: percentages
    complete, materials stored, milestones reached, asserted by the party requesting the money. On most
    private projects, no one independent ever checks the claim against the site before payment. The person
    certifying progress is paid by the party being paid.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the leverage is</h2>
  <p>
    Owner attention and owner leverage run in opposite directions. Leverage is highest before signatures,
    when terms can still be negotiated, bids can still be compared honestly, and walking away costs nothing
    but time. Attention peaks mid-construction, when a number arrives that doesn’t make sense — and by then
    the contract is fixed, the buyout is done, and the early draws are paid.
  </p>

  <RiskLadder />

  <p>
    Engaging help at month fourteen is not pointless; we do it regularly, and it pays. But it is corrective
    work, done without the leverage the paperwork gave away, and it costs more to achieve less. The same
    attention at month zero — on the contract, the bid comparison and the first three payment applications —
    is where this discipline earns most of its keep. That is why our entry engagement is a
    <a href="/services/pre-construction-audit">pre-construction audit</a> rather than a rescue product.
  </p>

  <h2>What good representation looks like day to day</h2>
  <p>
    Not inspiration — administration, done relentlessly. The representative chairs the project meetings and
    writes the minutes, because whoever writes the minutes decides what was agreed. They own the decision
    log, so no decision is made twice or unmade quietly. They read every payment application against work in
    place before it is approved, and sign or don’t. They interrogate every change order — in scope or new,
    priced against what support, doing what to the schedule. They keep the baseline honest, so slippage is a
    recorded fact with a date, not a dawning realization. And they escalate early, in writing, while the
    problem is still a conversation rather than a claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Any single week of it looks unremarkable. The compound effect is a project where the record is complete,
    the numbers are verified, and nobody performs differently when the owner isn’t looking — because someone
    is always looking.
  </p>

  <h2>What it isn’t</h2>
  <p>
    Not a second builder: we perform no construction work and hire no trades, and you contract directly with
    your general contractor. Not a project manager who reports to the GC — the entire point is that the
    obligation runs the other way. And not a guarantee. Things go wrong on well-represented projects too.
    Representation is a structure, not a promise: it determines how early you find out, how good your
    information is, and how strong your position is when something must be resolved.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs, and what it saves</h2>
  <p>
    Representation is a real cost, stated plainly on <a href="/fees">the fees page</a> — fixed and published
    for the audit, quoted per engagement for the rest. What it buys is measured in avoided cost, which is why
    we will never promise a savings figure: the overrun that didn’t happen has no receipt. What can be said
    honestly is where the avoided costs come from — terms improved before signing, draws checked before
    release, changes priced with support, disputes entered with a complete file — and that the largest of
    them concentrate early, which is why starting early costs less than starting late.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are weighing it, start smaller than a commitment: a <a href="/project-review">project review</a>
    costs nothing, and the audit is priced so that a serious owner can approve it without ceremony. Either
    way you will get our honest view — including, when it is true, that you don’t need us.
  </p>
